{"event":"call","n":1,"sent":"# Cheapest trips\n\nRead a file of roads between places and answer each trip question with the cheapest route.\n\n## Arguments\n\nThe program is run with exactly one argument: the name of the input file, which always exists and\ncan be read.\n\n## Input\n\nThe input file holds only the characters with codes 32 to 126 and the line feed (code 10). It is a\nsequence of lines: each line ends at a line feed, and the last line may lack one. An empty file has\nno lines. Lines are numbered from 1, blank lines included. The file has at most 200 lines, and no\nline is longer than 200 characters.\n\nA word is a run of characters other than the space, as long as possible. A name is 1 to 20\ncharacters, each a letter `A` to `Z` or `a` to `z`, a digit `0` to `9`, `_` or `-`. A line that\nholds no word is blank and is skipped. Every other line must hold exactly the words of one of:\n\n```\none FROM TO COST\ntwo FROM TO COST\ntrip FROM TO\n```\n\n`FROM` and `TO` are names and `COST` is 1 to 6 digits (`0` to `9`), leading zeros allowed: `007` is\n7 and `0` is 0.\n\n- `one FROM TO COST` is a road from `FROM` to `TO` that can be used only in that direction.\n- `two FROM TO COST` is two roads: one from `FROM` to `TO` and one from `TO` to `FROM`, each with\n  that cost.\n- `trip FROM TO` asks for the best route from `FROM` to `TO`.\n\nThe places are the names written on `one` and `two` lines. Several roads may join the same two\nplaces.\n\n## Routes\n\nA route from place `A` to place `B` is a sequence of roads in which the first starts at `A`, each\nnext one starts where the one before it ends, and the last ends at `B`. It may pass a place more\nthan once. Its cost is the sum of the costs of its roads. From a place to itself there is also the\nroute with no roads, of cost 0. A route is written as the places it visits, in order, `A` first and\n`B` last: one more place than it has roads.\n\nThe best route is the one with the lowest cost. When several routes have that cost, the best is the\none of them with the fewest roads. When several of those remain, compare their places one by one\nfrom the first: the best route is the one whose place comes first in ASCII order at the first place\nwhere they differ. A name that is the start of a longer name comes first.\n\nEach trip is answered with every road of the file, also the roads on lines after it.\n\n## Output\n\nPrint one line per trip, in file order. The line starts with `FROM to TO: ` and goes on with the\nfirst of these that applies:\n\n1. `unknown FROM` when `FROM` is not a place;\n2. `unknown TO` when `TO` is not a place;\n3. `no route` when there is no route;\n4. `cost C, R roads: P1 P2 ...` with `C` the cost and `R` the number of roads of the best route, in\n   decimal, then its places separated by single spaces. When `R` is 1 the word is `road`.\n\nEvery printed line ends with a line feed. The exit status is 0.\n\nExample input:\n\n```\ntwo home mill 4\none home bank 1\none bank mill 3\ntwo mill park 2\none home yard 5\ntrip home park\ntrip park bank\ntrip park park\ntrip home zoo\ntwo yard park 1\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```\nhome to park: cost 6, 2 roads: home mill park\npark to bank: cost 7, 3 roads: park mill home bank\npark to park: cost 0, 0 roads: park\nhome to zoo: unknown zoo\n```\n\nFrom `home` to `park` three routes cost 6: `home mill park` and `home yard park` with 2 roads, and\n`home bank mill park` with 3. Of the first two, `mill` comes before `yard`. The road from `yard` to\n`park` is on the last line and still counts.\n\n## Errors\n\nEach error prints exactly one line, ending with a line feed, and nothing else, and exits with\nstatus 20. `N` below is a line number in decimal.\n\nThe lines are checked in order from line 1, and only the first line with an error is reported:\n\n- The line is not blank and does not hold exactly the words of one of the three forms above:\n  `error: line N: bad line`\n- It is a `one` or `two` line whose `FROM` and `TO` are the same name: `error: line N: loop road`\n\nWhen every line is good but the file has no trip line, the error is `error: no trips`.\n\n## Visible cases\n\n### Case 1\n\nArguments: \"roads.txt\"\n\nFile `roads.txt`:\n```\ntwo home mill 4\none home bank 1\none bank mill 3\ntwo mill park 2\none home yard 5\ntrip home park\ntrip park bank\ntrip park park\ntrip home zoo\ntwo yard park 1\n```\n\nExpected standard output:\n```\nhome to park: cost 6, 2 roads: home mill park\npark to bank: cost 7, 3 roads: park mill home bank\npark to park: cost 0, 0 roads: park\nhome to zoo: unknown zoo\n```\n\nExpected exit status: 0\n\n### Case 2\n\nArguments: \"long.txt\"\n\nFile `long.txt`:\n```\ntrip a b\ntwo a b 1234567\n```\n\nExpected standard output:\n```\nerror: line 2: bad line\n```\n\nExpected exit status: 20\n","at":1792055831404}
{"event":"reply","n":1,"text":"Approach: parse lines, validate, and build the graph. Then, for each trip, run Dijkstra with a (cost, roads) key and recover the lexicographically smallest path.\n\nFor lexicographic tie-breaking, I compute dist and hops from the source with Dijkstra on (cost, hops) pairs. Then I compute for each node the best path to the target. To get the lexicographically smallest path, I compute from the target backwards: dist2[v] = best (cost,hops) from v to TO using reverse Dijkstra. Then from FROM, greedily walk: at node u, among edges u->v with cost(u,v)+d2[v] = d2[u] (cost and hops+1), choose the smallest name v. Since all edges are consistent with the optimal (cost,hops), the greedy walk gives the lexicographically smallest sequence. Zero-cost edges are fine because hops strictly decrease along the walk.\n\nName comparison is by ASCII with a prefix coming first, which is the default JS string comparison (by code unit).\n\nValidation: the `one`/`two` forms need exactly 4 words, `trip` needs 3. The cost must match `^[0-9]{1,6}$`, names must match `^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,20}$`. The line check goes in order, and the first error is reported (bad line vs. loop road per line). The no-trips error comes after all lines are good.\n\nLine splitting: split on \"\\n\"; if the file ends with a line feed, the final empty piece is just a blank line, which is harmless.\n\n```typescript\nimport * as fs from \"fs\";\n\nconst text = fs.readFileSync(process.argv[2], \"latin1\");\nconst lines = text.split(\"\\n\");\nconst nameRe = /^[A-Za-z0-9_-]{1,20}$/;\nconst costRe = /^[0-9]{1,6}$/;\ninterface E { to: string; c: number }\nconst adj = new Map<string, E[]>();\nconst radj = new Map<string, E[]>();\nconst trips: [string, string][] = [];\nfunction addPlace(n: string) {\n  if (!adj.has(n)) { adj.set(n, []); radj.set(n, []); }\n}\nfunction addRoad(a: string, b: string, c: number) {\n  adj.get(a)!.push({ to: b, c });\n  radj.get(b)!.push({ to: a, c });\n}\nfunction fail(msg: string): never {\n  process.stdout.write(msg + \"\\n\");\n  process.exit(20);\n}\nfor (let i = 0; i < lines.length; i++) {\n  const w = lines[i].split(\" \").filter((x) => x.length > 0);\n  if (w.length === 0) continue;\n  const n = i + 1;\n  if ((w[0] === \"one\" || w[0] === \"two\") && w.length === 4 &&\n      nameRe.test(w[1]) && nameRe.test(w[2]) && costRe.test(w[3])) {\n    if (w[1] === w[2]) fail(`error: line ${n}: loop road`);\n    const c = parseInt(w[3], 10);\n    addPlace(w[1]); addPlace(w[2]);\n    addRoad(w[1], w[2], c);\n    if (w[0] === \"two\") addRoad(w[2], w[1], c);\n  } else if (w[0] === \"trip\" && w.length === 3 && nameRe.test(w[1]) && nameRe.test(w[2])) {\n    trips.push([w[1], w[2]]);\n  } else {\n    fail(`error: line ${n}: bad line`);\n  }\n}\nif (trips.length === 0) fail(\"error: no trips\");\n\n// reverse Dijkstra from target on (cost,hops); graphs are small\nfunction rev(t: string): Map<string, [number, number]> {\n  const d = new Map<string, [number, number]>();\n  const done = new Set<string>();\n  d.set(t, [0, 0]);\n  while (true) {\n    let best: string | null = null;\n    let bd: [number, number] = [0, 0];\n    for (const [k, v] of d) {\n      if (done.has(k)) continue;\n      if (best === null || v[0] < bd[0] || (v[0] === bd[0] && v[1] < bd[1])) { best = k; bd = v; }\n    }\n    if (best === null) break;\n    done.add(best);\n    for (const e of radj.get(best)!) {\n      const nd: [number, number] = [bd[0] + e.c, bd[1] + 1];\n      const cur = d.get(e.to);\n      if (!cur || nd[0] < cur[0] || (nd[0] === cur[0] && nd[1] < cur[1])) d.set(e.to, nd);\n    }\n  }\n  return d;\n}\n\nconst out: string[] = [];\nfor (const [f, t] of trips) {\n  const pre = `${f} to ${t}: `;\n  if (!adj.has(f)) { out.push(pre + `unknown ${f}`); continue; }\n  if (!adj.has(t)) { out.push(pre + `unknown ${t}`); continue; }\n  const d = rev(t);\n  const s = d.get(f);\n  if (!s) { out.push(pre + \"no route\"); continue; }\n  const path = [f];\n  let u = f;\n  while (u !== t || d.get(u)![1] > 0) {\n    const du = d.get(u)!;\n    if (du[1] === 0) break;\n    let bestN: string | null = null;\n    for (const e of adj.get(u)!) {\n      const dv = d.get(e.to);\n      if (!dv) continue;\n      if (e.c + dv[0] === du[0] && dv[1] + 1 === du[1]) {\n        if (bestN === null || e.to < bestN) bestN = e.to;\n      }\n    }\n    if (bestN === null) break;\n    path.push(bestN);\n    u = bestN;\n  }\n  out.push(pre + `cost ${s[0]}, ${s[1]} ${s[1] === 1 ? \"road\" : \"roads\"}: ${path.join(\" \")}`);\n}\nprocess.stdout.write(out.join(\"\\n\") + \"\\n\");\n```","input":2487,"output":2014,"cut_off":false,"ms":12657,"at":1792055844070}
